00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
so functional training is perceived by  some people as a bit of a dirty word  that's because the term can apply to  almost anything  leaving it wide open to misappropriation  what does it even mean to be functional  well being functional for sports is very  different from being functional for your  general lifestyle functional training  varies from person to person  but assuming a conventional lifestyle we  can still apply this term usefully in  the past i've argued that our bodies  naturally adapt to our environments  which includes our habits and routines  so if your main function is sitting at a  desk typing then your body will adapt by  giving you rounded shoulders kyphosis  and tight hip flexors  so what's the use in balance training  and mobility training if you're never  going to use it anyway  well the problem is twofold firstly our  lifestyles are far more repetitive than  they ever have been before while  adapting to your routine is good and  fine it becomes a problem if you're  constantly in the same position doing  the exact same thing for example if your  wrists are constantly flexed as you type  and use a mouse you're more likely to  encounter elbow and wrist pain  eventually  repetitive strain injury is less about  repeating the same movement over and  over and more about not moving with  enough variety there is not a limit as  to how many times we can move a joint  before it wears out  this particular issue for example can  often be addressed with pronated wrist  curls which just balances out the  muscles  the other problem is that the  environments change and injury occurs  when the environment changes faster than  we can adapt if you only ever sit at the  desk typing or on the couch watching tv  you'll be ill prepared for the moment  when you do need to move furniture or  lift the kid that is when your back is  likely to go out  likewise if you never engage in any  routine cardio you're going to struggle  to chase that bus or play a team sport  does a conventional weightlifting  program or cardio routine solve all of  these issues  often not  firstly a strength routine or cardio  routine will only benefit the relevant  issues strength training won't help you  to climb those stairs for example unless  you focus on strength endurance as well  secondly these activities are somewhat  niche in their approach learning to lift  a barbell is very different from  learning to lift a log or a fence  many programs fail to prepare you for  rotational strength which is applying  strength in a transverse plane  likewise there'll be times when you need  to round your back to pick something up  we need to strengthen our bodies from a  wide variety of different positions to  be injury proof and to perform at our  very best  so just how functional are you let's  take a look at some questions you can  ask yourself and some ways you can make  your training more functional going  forwards  can you get into a resting squat  mobility is for many people a bigger  issue than strength if you can't get  into a resting squat this suggests tight  hips and or ankles and perhaps a weak  core there are other reasons you might  not be able to get into this position  but the vast majority of us should make  this a priority  can you touch your toes likewise an  inability to touch your toes suggests  tight hamstrings calves and other  posterior muscles this can create  tension throughout your entire posterior  chain contributing to a rounded back and  hunched shoulders and it can lead to  back pain in the lower back it should be  a key focus for those looking to improve  functional movement  can you raise your hands above your head  this is another position we should all  be able to get into comfortably fixing  this general mobility will go a long way  to preventing injury increasing freedom  of movement and boosting a sense of  vigour for a lot of people  to properly test this stand with your  back against the wall and try and raise  your hands to touch it and don't let  your back arch move away from the wall  in the process you might think you can  raise your hands over your head but try  this and you might be surprised just how  limited the range of motion really is  do you have good posture and normal gait  film yourself standing and walking are  you hunched over do your palms hang with  a neutral position are you lifting your  feet off the ground sufficiently do you  move symmetrically where appropriate  do you experience regular joint pain or  muscle pain some joint pain is  unavoidable owing to previous injury or  ongoing illness however joint pain and  muscle pain are not normal and shouldn't  be treated as such if you've just  accepted your knee pain as a part of who  you are now or as a part of getting  older you may be doing yourself a  disservice  addressing muscular imbalances or  tightnesses can often fix issues that at  one time seems like life sentences  i've seen people who've experienced  joint pain for years eventually overcome  it  getting injured from time to time is  fairly normal if you're very physically  active but you should be able to recover  reliably and not to make that part of  your identity chronic pain is not normal  do you regularly tweak your muscles this  is an issue i suffered with for a very  long time  despite being pretty strong and fit on  paper i often put my back out during  mundane activities this could have been  an issue with mobility weaknesses in  smaller muscles or even proprioception  and movement patterns  for me lots of downward dog and more  rotational training has seemingly solved  this issue fingers crossed  do you get out of breath climbing the  stairs or running for the bus  if you regularly get out of breath then  you likely need to increase your cardio  even if you're incredibly strong if you  pant after climbing the stairs i  personally struggle to think of that as  being ideally fit  can you run and jump you should be able  to maintain a short jog sprint at full  speed without anything hurting and jump  off the ground with decent form not only  these fundamental human movements but  they might also someday save your life  can you perform a single push-up and  pull-up  barring atypical injuries or illnesses  most people should aspire to being able  to do a single push-up and pull up well  into old age this is a good indication  of your strength to weight ratio and  also requires a decent amount of control  being able to perform a pull-up also  means you should be able to pull  yourself up onto a ledge which again  could be a life-saving movement  can you lift 30 kilograms from the  ground and carry it  i thought on this for a while how much  should a person be able to lift off the  ground i'd love to hear your thoughts on  this in the comments down below  and should it be relative to their body  weight well i massively believe in  strength to weight ratio the truth is  that the world doesn't change when you  get heavier if you can lift 30 kilograms  you can lift most shopping most  suitcases and most small children and  this should be very achievable for most  people to aim for  again barring any injuries or illnesses  as well as lifting though you should  also be able to carry this object for a  short distance  after all in reality we are rarely  tasked with lifting something up and  then putting it immediately back down  can you balance on one leg if you can't  balance on one leg you'll be  significantly more likely to fall and  hurt yourself and limited in terms of  many other movements  do you feel constantly tired if you feel  sluggish and tired 90 of the time you  likely aren't working at your best  and you likely aren't extremely fit  either many people try to work out how  to get more energy they search for  things like why am i always tired but  they often neglect the simplest factor  of all exercise training will improve  everything from your vo2 max to your  stroke volume to your mitochondria  you'll sleep better too so if you're  otherwise fit consider speaking to your  doctor but consider first whether just  adding some more cardio into your  training could help a lot  can you focus on a task the brain can be  trained just like the body and just like  the body it can atrophy with misuse if  you struggle to focus on a task you may  need to consider interventions  can you rapidly switch between tasks  another example of cognitive fitness is  being able to rapidly switch between  tasks again your life could depend on  your ability to react quickly to a  changing situation  so that's just a small list of ideas  just some things that i came up with off  the top of my head i'd love to hear your  ideas in the comments down below  physiotherapists and other professionals  use a battery of tests to ascertain  basic balance coordination and range of  motion but as a quick and dirty way to  assess whether you meet basic fitness  requirements this list could be fairly  useful but now you've identified the  challenges you currently face what can  you do to address them any training is  better than nothing if you're running or  lifting weights regularly then you're  already doing great if not time to start  or alternatively take up a hobby like  rock climbing swimming walking or sports  or better yet a combination of those  but you'd be surprised just how many  people who are very strong or very fit  in some ways actually fail a number of  these tests so from there it's a matter  of looking at what's missing from your  current training if you're a power  lifter you might benefit from adding  some cardio and rotational strength  running and training with kettlebells  can offer these things  a runner might benefit from picking up  some weights to train their upper body  every form of training in every modality  offers its own benefits but by combining  these ideas from different places we'll  create a more well-rounded fitness  profile you might progress in each  activity a little more slowly than you  otherwise would have done you may not  hit that double body weight squat quite  so quickly but for the average person i  would argue it's more useful to be able  to move pain-free  i talk about super functional training  to try and address some of these issues  if being functional means being fit for  your daily activities being super  functional means being fit in ways you  don't necessarily need to be on the  regular because a sometimes you might  need that kind of fitness and b because  it's cool  as one viewer perfectly described  functional training is bruce wayne  training to be batman  but super functional training is  everyone else training to be batman  don't settle for good enough train to be  the best you can be physically and  mentally in every regard once you can  handle these basics it's time to set  bigger goals instead of push-ups how  about handstand push-ups instead of  balancing on one leg how about squatting  on a railing  of course if you like the idea of  training to be more functional or even  super functional you'll enjoy my ebook  and training program super functional  training that book is all about training  to improve not just your strength but  also your mobility your endurance and  even cognitive function all in a  well-rounded and balanced manner it's  linked in the description down below  anyway thank you so much for watching  this one guys if you found it useful or  interesting then please consider leaving  a like as that would help me out  immensely subscribe if you want more  like this and hit the bell button for  notifications either way thanks again  and  bye for now

